{=============================================================================
  Centrifugal air compressor of a gas turbine (exercise 5.14)
  CoolSolve Library model CSL-0213
 -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
  Steady-flow energy balance on an adiabatic centrifugal air compressor.
  The change in potential energy and the inlet kinetic energy are neglected,
  as in the original. The power includes the outlet kinetic energy. rapW is
  the share of that power that goes into the enthalpy rise.
  Teaching exercise 5.14, ULiège MECA0002, thermodynamique appliquée,
  repetition 3 (2022-2023).

  Inputs : p_in, T_in, p_out, T_out, c_out, q_m
  Outputs: W_dot, h_in, h_out, rapW
  Units  : SI - temperatures in K, pressures in Pa, energies in J
           (property calls receive °C)
  Source : ULiège MECA0002, R3_E5_2022.EES; the file names no author - see README.md
=============================================================================}

"!Data"
"Exercise 5, as in the original: the centrifugal air compressor of a gas turbine receives air from the ambient atmosphere, where the pressure is 1 bar and the temperature is 300 K. At the compressor outlet the pressure is 4 bar, the temperature is 480 K and the velocity is 100 m/s. The mass flow rate at the compressor inlet is 15 kg/s. Calculate the power required to drive the compressor."
p_in = 100e3 [Pa]                              "inlet pressure [Pa], 100 kPa in the original"
T_in = 300 [K]                                 "inlet temperature [K], as stored; kept in kelvin"
p_out = 400e3 [Pa]                             "outlet pressure [Pa], 400 kPa in the original"
T_out = 480 [K]                                "outlet temperature [K], as stored; kept in kelvin"
c_out = 100 [m/s]                              "outlet velocity [m/s]"
q_m = 15 [kg/s]                                "inlet mass flow rate [kg/s]"

"!Steady-flow energy balance"
"First law for an open system in steady state, as in the original: Q_dot + W_dot = q_m_out*(h_out + c_out^2/2 + g*z_out) - q_m_in*(h_in + c_in^2/2 + g*z_in). Mass conservation gives q_m_out = q_m_in. Q_dot = 0 (the compressor is assumed adiabatic). The potential-energy change is neglected. The inlet kinetic energy is neglected because the control volume is large enough that the air speed across the inlet surface is very small."
W_dot = q_m*(h_out - h_in + c_out^2/2)        "compressor power [W]; work received by the system is positive, as in the original"
h_out = enthalpy(Air, T=T_out-273.15)         "outlet specific enthalpy [J/kg]; the call takes °C and T_out is in K"
h_in = enthalpy(Air, T=T_in-273.15)           "inlet specific enthalpy [J/kg]; the call takes °C and T_in is in K"

rapW = 100[%]*q_m*(h_out - h_in)/W_dot        "share of the compressor power that goes into the enthalpy rise [%]"
"About 97.4% of the power supplied by the compressor goes into the enthalpy transfer. Only 2.6% of the power is converted into kinetic energy, as in the original."
